<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Walid II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 706–<a href="/years/744">744</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Walid II was the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 743 to 744. A gifted poet known for his wine songs and worldly life, he succeeded his uncle Hisham but quickly alienated powerful members of the family and the Yaman tribal faction through his treatment of rivals and favour to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>. He was overthrown and killed in April 744 by supporters of his cousin Yazid III, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-fitna">Third Fitna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-walid-ii.jpg" alt="Al-Walid II" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taha b. Wasiq b. Hussain · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyad_fresco_of_Prince_(future_caliph)_Walid_bin_Yazid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Walid ibn Yazid was the son of the Umayyad caliph Yazid II, who ruled until January 724, and of Zaynab bint Muhammad al-Thaqafi. Yazid had intended him to succeed at once but was persuaded by his brother Maslama to name his half-brother Hisham instead, with al-Walid second in the line of succession, a place he had been given at the age of eleven.</p><p>Hisham at first regarded his nephew with affection but grew disillusioned with his dissolute lifestyle, reduced his allowances and punished some of his associates. Al-Walid withdrew from the court to a remote desert residence, perhaps the ruined site now known as Qasr Tuba in the Jordanian desert. After the pilgrimage of 735 Hisham tried to persuade him to step down in favour of Hisham's own son Maslama, or to swear allegiance to Maslama as his successor, and al-Walid refused. Hisham secretly gathered support for Maslama, but the death of the general Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik in the late 730s was a major setback to the plan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Al-Walid succeeded on the death of Hisham on 6 February 743. As heir he had been known for open-handedness, and as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> he took special care of the crippled and blind, increasing their stipend. On 21 May 743 he named his two sons, al-Hakam and Uthman, to succeed him in that order. He ordered the arrest of Hisham's sons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusafa">Rusafa</a>, but forbade any harm to Maslama and his household in memory of their old companionship. Al-Tabari quotes a number of his poems, and he was fond of versifying and of horse races.</p><p>His reputed drinking, singing and immorality aroused opposition, and he imprisoned Sulayman ibn Hisham. When Khalid ibn Abdallah al-Qasri, approached by plotters, declined to join and cautioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, al-Walid's anger was aroused; he imprisoned Khalid and handed him to Yusuf ibn Umar for fifty million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a>, and Yusuf tortured and killed him, which intensely angered many of al-Walid's relatives. Yazid ibn al-Walid spoke against al-Walid's favour to the Qaysi Arabs over Yemenis and non-Arab Muslims, and drew support from the Qadariyyah and Murji'ah. Marwan ibn Muhammad wrote from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> urging prudence, but was disregarded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Yazid slipped into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and deposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in a coup, then distributed money from the treasury. Al-Walid was besieged in a castle outside the city. He fought well but in April 744, at Al-Aghdaf, in what is now Jordan, he was defeated and killed by the forces of Sulayman ibn Hisham. His cousin Yazid III succeeded him and imprisoned his two sons. According to Yazid's own account, al-Walid rejected an offer to set up a tribal assembly to decide the future of the realm and attacked.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Walid II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
